\section{The join key is the namespace, not the prefix}{

Filers bind namespace prefixes freely -- \code{d}, \code{e}, \code{f}, \code{g},
\code{EOGS_202040000} all appear -- and the same letter means different
namespaces in different filings. Joining on the prefix looks equivalent and
silently attaches the wrong element definition, so the join runs on the
namespace URI.
}

\section{Parent company versus group}{

Consolidated figures are tagged with a member of
\code{ConsolidatedSoloDimension}. Parent-company figures are \strong{not tagged at
all}, and reading their absence as "parent" is only legitimate because the
taxonomy declares a default member for that dimension. This function reads
the declared default rather than assuming one, and records in
\code{basis_source} whether the filing said so (\code{"tagged"}) or the default
decided it (\code{"default"}) -- a figure that is the parent's only by default is
worth being able to find again.
}

\section{Codes}{

The code comes from the taxonomy's own numbering: the role URI gives the
statement (\code{.../role/400.00/BalanceSheetAccountForm}) and the presentation
arcs' \code{order} values give the levels, so \verb{400.00.020.010.020} is
"Assets, non-current, property plant and equipment" and sorts into statement
order on its own.
}

\section{Nothing is dropped}{

A fact keeps its row whether or not the taxonomy has anything to say about
it. Two kinds do not:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Facts in no statement} -- company data, the management's review, the
auditor's report. They are the whole of what \code{\link[=xbrl_company]{xbrl_company()}} reads, so
dropping them would mean reading every filing twice. They come back with
\code{statement} \code{NA}.
\item \strong{Facts whose taxonomy version is unknown}, because the filing declares
an entry point newer than the packaged tables. Every taxonomy column is
\code{NA} and one warning names the version, so a filing from a taxonomy this
release has not caught up with degrades to its raw facts instead of
disappearing. \code{\link[=read_taxonomy]{read_taxonomy()}} on the published package fixes it.
}
